• Read brain-star-map/scripts/supervise-all-agents.js, brain-star-map/scripts/start-supervisor.vbs, brain-star-map/scripts/start-router-at-logon.vbs, brain-star-map/package.json, brain-star-map/.gitignore
Design a safe cross-platform restart script for this Windows-first Node project. It supervises 25 Blocks agents using blocks/logs/supervisor.pid, per-agent *.watchdog.pid, and *.child.pid. Existing supervisor handles graceful SIGTERM, Windows requires taskkill /PID /T /F to avoid orphaning blocks children. The script should stop only PIDs recorded by this project, clean stale markers, launch scripts/supervise-all-agents.js detached, support --dry-run/help, and avoid printing secrets. Review relevant files and flag edge cases or minimal implementation recommendations.
• Read brain-star-map/scripts/supervise-all-agents.js, brain-star-map/scripts/watch-blocks-agent.js, brain-star-map/scripts/start-supervisor.vbs, brain-star-map/package.json, .vscode/settings.json
Here is the recommended approach for the cross-platform restart script, designed as a Node script (e.g.,  scripts/restart.js ) to match the stack.
Recommended Implementation Logic
1. CLI Parsing: Intercept  --help  (print usage, exit) and  --dry-run  (flag for logging-only mode).
2. PID Discovery: Scan  blocks/logs/  for  supervisor.pid ,  *.watchdog.pid , and  *.child.pid . Read and deduplicate the integer PIDs.
3. Targeted Shutdown Order:
- Crucial: Kill the Supervisor first. If you kill a watchdog first, the living supervisor will immediately schedule a restart.
- Kill Watchdogs second.
- Kill Children last (catch any orphans).
4. Cross-Platform Kill & Clean:
- Filter out  process.pid .
- Windows: Use  spawnSync('taskkill', ['/PID', pid, '/T', '/F']) .
- Unix: Use  process.kill(pid, 'SIGKILL')  (since you want a guaranteed stop before restart).
- Delete the  .pid  files using  fs.unlinkSync() .
5. Detached Relaunch:
- Windows:  spawn('wscript', ['scripts/start-supervisor.vbs'], { detached: true, stdio: 'ignore' }) 
- Unix:  spawn(process.execPath, ['scripts/supervise-all-agents.js'], { detached: true, stdio: 'ignore' }) 
- Call  unref()  on the spawned child and exit the restart script.
Edge Cases & Recommendations to Flag
- Stale PID OS Reuse (Danger): If an agent crashed hard and the OS reused its PID for a system process, blinding killing it is dangerous. Recommendation: Before killing, verify the process is a Node/Blocks process using  tasklist /FI "PID eq <pid>"  (Windows) or  ps -p <pid>  (Unix).
- Double-Kill Logs:  taskkill /T /F  on a parent inherently kills its children. If you run  taskkill  on a watchdog, its child PID might already be dead by the time you process the  *.child.pid  file. Ignore  ESRCH  (Unix) or  taskkill  exit code 128 (Windows).
- Secret Leakage: Ensure your dry-run console logs only output the PIDs, file names, and command strings. Never pass or spread  process.env  into the  console.log  statements.
- Race Condition: Wait a brief moment (e.g.,  setTimeout  for 1-2 seconds) between killing the processes and launching the new supervisor to ensure OS port locks and file locks on  blocks/logs/*.log  are fully released.
The existing supervisor already has the right crash-recovery behavior, so the restart command should restart the supervisor tree, not kill  blocks.exe  globally. I’ll add a targeted Node script that uses only this project’s PID files, supports  --dry-run / --help , handles Windows and Unix-like systems, and relaunches the supervisor detached.
