Review the new brain-star-map/scripts/restart-agents.js and package.json blocks:restart command. Focus on process-safety, Windows behavior, PID reuse, duplicate supervisor prevention, stale PID handling, and whether the script could unexpectedly kill unrelated processes. Report actionable issues only.
Actionable issues:
- High: PID reuse remains unsafe. Validate the executable path/command line against this project ( supervise-all-agents.js ,  watch-blocks-agent.js , or the expected Blocks binary), not just  node / blocks  image names. The current check could kill an unrelated Node/Blocks process whose PID was reused.
- Medium: Unix shutdown is racy. The script waits 500 ms, but the supervisor’s own graceful shutdown waits 1.5 seconds. Poll recorded PIDs until they exit, with a bounded timeout, before launching the replacement supervisor.
- Medium:  start-supervisor.vbs  contains hard-coded  C:\Users\dividicus\...  and Node paths. Calling it from the new script can restart a different/stale checkout—or fail entirely—when moved to another host. Make the VBS derive its own directory, or launch the computed  ROOT / SUPERVISOR  path directly.
- Low: The post-launch 1.5-second verification can produce a false failure on a slow machine. Poll for the supervisor PID with a timeout rather than using one fixed sleep.
The review found three real edge cases, so I’m tightening the implementation before calling it complete:
- verify recorded Node PIDs by command line, not only by  node.exe ;
- wait for the old supervisor tree to fully exit instead of using a fixed short delay;
- remove the hard-coded machine path from the Windows launcher and make it derive its repository location.
